p.src:
The next question is whether the same system has a Hamiltonian description. Begin with $\begin{aligned}
  p_x&=m\dot x+\frac{e}{c}A_x,\\
  p_y&=m\dot y+\frac{e}{c}A_y,\\
  p_z&=m\dot z+\frac{e}{c}A_z.\end{aligned}$ These relations can be inverted: $\begin{aligned}
  \dot x&=\frac{1}{m}\left(p_x-\frac{e}{c}A_x\right),\\
  \dot y&=\frac{1}{m}\left(p_y-\frac{e}{c}A_y\right),\\
  \dot z&=\frac{1}{m}\left(p_z-\frac{e}{c}A_z\right).\end{aligned}$ The Legendre transform is $H=\sum_i p_i\dot x_i-L.$ Substitution and cancellation produce $\boxed{
  H(\mathbf x,\mathbf p)
  =
  \frac{1}{2m}
  \left(
    \mathbf p-\frac{e}{c}\mathbf A(\mathbf x)
  \right)^2
  }.$

